    "response_json": {
      "answer": "The Stoics conceptualized fate as Heimarmenê (Εἱμαρμένη) [4, 6] and Providentia (Providence) [1], maintaining that while the universe is governed by these forces, individuals retain moral responsibility through specific faculties. Central to this is the doctrine of 'tò eph' hēmin' (what is in our power) [2, 4]. Epictetus defined this through 'Prohairesis' (Προαίρεσις), or deliberate choice, which allows for moral agency within a fated system [2]. Marcus Aurelius similarly emphasized the 'Hegemonikon' (ἡγεμονικόν), or ruling faculty, as the seat of individual reason [3]. To reconcile universal fate with responsibility, Chrysippus of Soli introduced a distinction between 'Perfect' and 'Antecedent' causes [9, 10]. This framework supports the 'Willing Assent to Fate' (Praebere se Fato) argument, particularly in the Imperial tradition of Seneca, who viewed adversity as 'Exercitatio' (training) rather than mere misfortune [1]. The Stoic position is a key component of the ancient debate between compatibilism and incompatibilism [7, 8], with Cleanthes of Assos providing foundational arguments such as the 'Hymn to Zeus' to illustrate the alignment of the human will with divine fate [4].",
      "confidence": 0.8253511434634999,
      "processingTime": 132042
    }
  },
  "arm_b_new_system": {
    "answer": "- Synkatathesis (Stoic Assent, συγκατάθεσις): The Stoic concept of mental assent (synkatathesis) to an impression (phantasia), central to what modern scholars characterize as Chrysippus's compatibilist account of determinism and moral responsibility. [...] [1]\n- Stoic Compatibilism and Moral Responsibility: Scholarly debate on how Stoics reconciled their determinism with moral responsibility. Key questions: Is Stoic ethics compatibilist? How does the distinction between \"up to us\" (eph' hemin) and fate work? [...] [2]\n- Stoic-Academic Debate on Fate: Central Hellenistic debate between Stoics (esp. Chrysippus) and Academic Skeptics (esp. Carneades) on fate, determinism, and moral responsibility [3]\n- Alexander's Incompatibilist Argument (Fate Destroys Freedom): Alexander's argument (characterized in modern scholarship as incompatibilist) is the definitive ancient statement that universal determinism (Stoic heimarmenê/fate) is incompatible with genuine free will. [...] [4]\n- Boethius d. 524 addresses the question in De consolatione philosophiae at Cons. 104 [P1]\nOriginal: \"— Nimis quidem, inquam, vera ratio et, si quam paulo ante docuisti providentiam fatumve {fatumve} considerem firmis viribus nixa sententia. Sed eam si placet, inter eas, quas inopinabiles paulo ante posuisti, numeremus. — Qui? [...]\" [P1]\n- Marcus Tullius Cicero addresses the question in De Fato at Fat. 11 [P2]\nOriginal: \"Sed haec ex naturalibus causis vitia nasci possunt, extirpari autem et funditus tolli, ut is ipse, qui ad ea propensus fuerit, a tantis vitiis avocetur, non est id positum in naturalibus causis, sed in voluntate, studio, disciplina. [...]\" [P2]\n- Marcus Tullius Cicero addresses the question in De Fato at Fat. 13 [P3]\nOriginal: \"At hoc, Chrysippe, minime vis, maximeque tibi de hoc ipso cum Diodoro certamen est. [...]\" [P3]\n- Marcus Tullius Cicero addresses the question in De Fato at Fat. 21 [P4]\nOriginal: \"Quod si ita est, omnia, quae fiunt, causis fiunt antegressis; id si ita est, fato omnia fiunt; [...]\" [P4]",
